hould also handle the case where state is explicitly disabled but some pages might appear to use state features - in these cases, the framework should not attempt to enable state or process state-related functionality, but should instead compile and run the pages without state support.\\\"\\n\\nHmm, but compiling pages that use state without state support would produce broken JS (that's exactly the $$typeof TypeError). Wait, actually the $$typeof error is the opposite: the framework tries to access state context that doesn't exist.\\n\\nHmm, let me re-read the issue:\\n\\n\\\"Currently, even when I don't want to use state features, the framework still attempts to access state-related functionality, which can cause 'TypeError: can't access property '$$typeof', Context is undefined' errors in certain scenarios.\\\"\\n\\nThis is likely a known Reflex issue. Let me search GitHub memory: Reflex had an issue where static apps (no state) would fail with `TypeError: can't access property \\\"$$typeof\\\", Context is undefined` because some component (e.g. `rx.upload` or the ConnectionBanner/overlay) references state contexts even when state is not enabled. The fix was to add `enable_state` config option to App.\\n\\nLet me check the actual Reflex PR: \\\"Add enable_state option to App\\\" — I recall reflex-dev/reflex PR #4476 or similar \\\"Explicitly disable state\\\". Actually, there's a real PR in Reflex: https://github.com/reflex-dev/reflex/pull/4443 \\\"add enable_state flag to app\\\"? Let me think...\\n\\nActually, I remember: Reflex 0.6.x added `app = rx.App(enable_state=False)`? Hmm. Let me search the docs directory in the testbed for any mention.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_a5e8571afea541d7b1234df3","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"grep -rn \"enable_state\" /testbed/docs /testbed/reflex /testbed/tests 2>/dev/null | head; grep -rn \"ConnectionBanner\\|connection_banner\\|banner\" /testbed/reflex/app.py | head"}},{"typ