 args were already all-optional; adding a new field in the middle changes positional positions but that's what upstream did. Most users use kwargs. Fine — match upstream exactly.\\n\\nHmm, actually wait. Let me double check one thing about `__post_init__` change. Current:\\n\\n```python\\n        self._setup_state()\\n```\\n\\nNew:\\n```python\\n        if self.enable_state:\\n            self._enable_state()\\n```\\n\\nWith enable_state=True: `_enable_state()` → `_state = State` (if not already set via deprecated `state` kwarg — note `_state` is init arg `state`? Let me check: the deprecated `state` kwarg maps to `_state`. In `test_raise_on_state`: `App(_state=State)`. OK.) → `_setup_state()`.\\n\\nWith enable_state=False: nothing.\\n\\nBut hmm — there's an ordering issue in `__post_init__`: `_setup_state` uses `get_config()` and sets up sio etc. Previously for stateful apps this ran at init; now same for enable_state=True. For enable_state=False apps, no socket — matches \\\"The application to skip all state initialization and setup processes during app initialization\\\".\\n\\nAlso `test_app_state_manager` expects `app.state_manager` to raise ValueError when not set. Let me confirm the property:\\n\\nLet me check `state_manager` property in app.py.\\n\\nAlso `modify_state` and websocket handlers use `self.state_manager` — but with enable_state=False, the socket isn't mounted so no requests hit them. `_add_optional_endpoints` adds upload endpoint — uses state_manager? Only when Upload.is_used... The upload endpoint calls `app.state_manager`? Let me check quickly. Actually in upstream this was fine, so don't over-engineer.\\n\\nLet me now also check `test_app.py` current line numbers for the sections the diff touches so I can confirm after checkout. Also there's `tests/units/test_app.py:1523: app._enable_state()` in test_app_state_manager — stays (app = App(enable_state=False) then `app._enable_state()`).\\n\\nOK let me proceed. Actually, one more consideration — maybe I shouldn't use git checkout blindly; instead let me carefully apply each change with the edit tool for source files (app.py, compiler.py), and use git for tests. Hmm. The instruction says use edit for precise changes; write for complete rewrites. git checkout is a bash command — allowed (\\\"Use bash for file operations\\\"). I think the pragmatic approach: apply source changes via edit (so I control exactly what changes), and test changes via git checkout (large JSX strings). But then test file at d324b25c is HEAD+commit for that file — verified. \\n\\nActually you know what, let me just do all six files via edit/git as appropriate:\\n\\n- reflex/app.py: edit tool (6 small edits)\\n- reflex/compiler/compiler.py: edit tool (4 edits)\\n- tests/units/test_app.py: git checkout (big JSX changes) — then verify with git diff that the diff matches the upstream test diff exactly.\\n- tests/benchmarks/test_compilation.py: edit (1 line)\\n- tests/integration/test_tailwind.py: edit\\n- tests/integration/tests_playwright/test_stateless_app.py: edit\\n\\nThen run tests.\\n\\nLet me start with app.py edits.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"text","text":"I now have the complete picture. The upstream fix (PR #5583) does the following:\n\n1. **`App` gains an `enable_state: bool = True` flag** — when `False`, all state setup is skipped at init\n2. **`_enable_state()`** always calls `_setup_state()` when invoked (so init can rely on it)\n3. **`_compile_page`** no longer auto-detects stateful pages / auto-enables state\n4. **The compiler** (`_compile_page`, `compile_page`, `compile_unevaluated_page`, `ExecutorSafeFunctions`) no longer threads state through or returns an `enable_state` flag\n\nLet me apply the changes to `reflex/app.py` first:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_01491ed193774ac2ad2073c2","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"/testbed/reflex/app.py","edits":[{"oldText":"    # The state class to use for the app.\n    _state: type[BaseState] | None = None\n